// **a `T&` a call returned is read as a value.** `at()` is documented as the spelling to prefer over
// the bracket, and until AST::read_reaches_storage was the shared answer it could not be read at all:
// only a place got the deref AST::PointerAdjuster writes, and a call is not a place. so
// `int32 $v = $a->at(0);` was "cannot implicitly convert 'int32&' to 'int32'" - a documented API that
// worked in four positions and not in the obvious fifth
//
// the sharper half was silent. with no deref the operand stayed a pointer, so `$a->at(1) + 1` reached
// the *pointer* arm of AST::binary_has_builtin_meaning and emitted a getelementptr - an address one
// element along, printed as a number

array<int32> $a = [4, 8, 15];

int32 $v = $a->at(0);
echo $v;

echo $a->at(1);

// **the number is the assertion.** a GEP by one element over an int32 would print the address plus
// four, so 9 is the whole of what says this is an integer add
echo $a->at(1) + 1;

// a borrow destination still binds rather than reads - as_value_for's pointer-shaped arm, untouched -
// and writing through it still writes into the array
int32& $b = $a->at(2);
$b = 99;
echo $a[2];

// and the read is a read: the array is unchanged by taking a value out of it
echo $a->at(0);
echo $a->count();
